{% extends "specification_user.jinja2" %}
{% block requirements %}
Create the Business Rules in English. Use rule identifiers such as BR-001, preferring existing source identifiers. Organize shared conditions across screens, APIs, data, and batch jobs into consistent rules.

## Rule Inventory and Scope
State rule IDs, names, purposes, applicable targets, actors and permissions, timing, and evidence.
## Decisions, Calculations, and State Transitions
Define prerequisites, inputs, decision conditions, results, formulas, rounding, units, boundary values, priorities, conflict handling, and effective periods.
Use decision tables where appropriate and cover all business conditions in the source, including approvals, access control, period-end processing, and prohibited operations.
## Application Points and Validation Conditions
Map each rule to its enforcing screens, APIs, or batch jobs and the data it reads or updates using identifiers. State validation conditions for satisfied, unsatisfied, and boundary cases.
## Consistency and Open Questions
Identify conflicting rules, unresolved thresholds, calculation conditions or priorities, proposals carried from preceding specifications, and source references.
{% endblock %}
